content:
The basic idea is a horizontal track inside a viewport that only reveals one slide. Changing slides means translating the entire track left or right.

The HTML has three layers: `.carousel` provides the positioning context, `.carousel__viewport` clips overflow, and `.carousel__track` contains three `.carousel__slide` elements. Previous/next buttons sit at the sides, while JavaScript creates the navigation dots.

The important SCSS is:

```scss
.carousel__viewport { overflow: hidden; }
.carousel__track {
  display: flex;
  transition: transform $dur-slow $ease-inout;
}
.carousel__slide { flex: 0 0 100%; }
```

Every slide is one viewport width. JavaScript only stores an index and renders it by moving the track:

```js
let index = 0;
track.style.transform = `translate3d(-${index * 100}%, 0, 0)`;
```

The browser performs the animation through the CSS transition. No pixel width is stored, so resizing the viewport does not break alignment. Navigation wraps with modular arithmetic:

```js
function goTo(target) {
  index = (target + slides.length) % slides.length;
  render();
}
```

The arrows, dots, and keyboard handlers all call `goTo()`. Dots are generated from the number of slides, and `aria-hidden`/`tabIndex` are updated so keyboard focus does not enter off-screen content.

[tools used: Bash]

The position indicator answers: which section is the user currently reading? The probe line is placed immediately below the navbar. The active section is the last section whose top edge has crossed that line.

The key API is:

```js
section.getBoundingClientRect().top
```

It returns the section's current top position relative to the viewport. A section still below the viewport has a large positive value; one that has passed above it has a negative value. The code pairs each navigation link with the section named by its hash, ignores missing targets defensively, and scans them in document order:

```js
const probe = navHeight() + 1;
let current = null;

targets.forEach(({ link, section }) => {
  if (section.getBoundingClientRect().top <= probe) current = link;
});

setActive(current);
```

The loop intentionally keeps overwriting `current`, because the last matching section is the lowest section that has crossed the probe. The hero has no navigation link, so no item is active before the first linked section reaches the probe.

The bottom of the page needs a special case:

```js
if (scrollY + viewport >= docHeight - 2) {
  setActive(targets[targets.length - 1].link);
  return;
}
```

Without it, a short final section may never reach the probe even though the user cannot scroll farther. The two-pixel tolerance handles fractional-pixel rounding. The navbar height is read dynamically because it changes from 88px to 56px when the navbar shrinks.

The scroll callback is throttled with `requestAnimationFrame`, ensuring that repeated scroll events produce at most one layout calculation per animation frame. The listener is passive. JavaScript only toggles `.is-active`; SCSS draws the animated underline with a pseudo-element and `transform: scaleX()`.

[tools used: Bash]

I redesigned the Film section. The browser `controls` attribute was removed, and the layout changed from a narrow centered portrait video to a balanced two-column composition with explanatory text on the left and the video on the right. A blurred red radial glow visually connects it to the source footage and the page's accent color.

Playback uses pointer, keyboard, and touch-compatible events:

```js
frame.addEventListener('mouseenter', play);
frame.addEventListener('mouseleave', pause);
frame.addEventListener('focus', play);
frame.addEventListener('blur', pause);
frame.addEventListener('click', toggle);
```

A translucent overlay displays a play icon and `Hover to play`, then fades while the video is playing. On devices without hover, a media query changes the text to `Tap to play`. The Promise returned by `video.play()` is caught because a browser may reject playback.

Testing confirmed that hover starts playback, leaving pauses it, the controls are absent, the portrait layout stacks and centers at 768x1024, and all five required viewport sizes still have zero horizontal overflow. The section still uses a standard `<video>` with an MP4 `<source>`, so it satisfies the HTML5 video requirement.

[tools used: Bash, Read]

I implemented the color selector so the four swatches change the entire Order section's theme and update the displayed finish name.

JavaScript changes only a semantic data attribute:

```js
section.dataset.finish = chosen.dataset.finish;
```

The color values remain in SCSS:

```scss
.order {
  --finish-bg: #0f0f12;
  --finish-glow: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  background-color: var(--finish-bg);
  transition: background-color 620ms;

  &[data-finish="natural"] { --finish-bg: #1a1510; }
  &[data-finish="crimson"] { --finish-bg: #1e0b11; }
  &[data-finish="sky"] { --finish-bg: #0c131d; }
}
```

This avoids assigning colors through `element.style`, gives `background-color` a smooth transition, and provides a clear use of CSS variables. A pseudo-element supplies the glow because gradients themselves do not interpolate reliably.

The finish name uses `aria-live="polite"`, each swatch synchronizes `aria-pressed`, and exactly one option remains selected. Testing confirmed all four computed background colors and names, no inline style on the Order section, no horizontal overflow at the required resolutions, and no inline scripts or table layouts.

[tools used: Bash, Read]
